    },
    "*\"There's this assumption that if you have a stoma you won't be able to have a baby,\"* says Holly Fleet, 28, who faced pregnancy after an emergency ileostomy in 2021. \nThe reality is very different: the vast majority of women with stomas can conceive and experience completely normal pregnancies. Stomas don't affect fertility, so conception occurs without additional problems.\n\n**First Trimester: Managing Nausea**\nDuring the first months, morning sickness requires specific strategies. Keep dry crackers and water always within reach to manage discomfort. \nIt's preferable to change the collection system in the evening when nausea is less intense, and use smaller bags during the day for greater comfort. Hydration control becomes fundamental and requires regular medical monitoring.\n\n**Second and Third Trimester: Physical Adaptations**\nAs the belly grows, stoma size may increase but will return to normal after delivery. Use more flexible baseplates that adapt to body changes and invest in support belts specific for pregnant ostomates. The stoma may disappear from view as the belly grows, so using a mirror becomes helpful. Apply barrier creams to protect more sensitive skin and document changes with photos to help the medical team.\n\n**Delivery and Breastfeeding**\nMost women deliver without stoma-related complications. It's essential to prepare extra hospital supplies and inform all healthcare staff about the stoma presence. \nDuring breastfeeding, experimenting with different positions helps find the most comfortable one. Often the side-lying position avoids pressure on the stoma while maintaining good baby latch.\n\nHaving a stoma is not an obstacle to motherhood. With proper preparation and medical support, every woman can fully experience pregnancy and parenthood, often discovering unexpected strength and resilience." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "*\"There's this assumption that if you have a stoma you won't be able to have a baby,\"* says Holly Fleet, 28, who faced pregnancy after an emergency ileostomy in 2021. \nThe reality is very different: the vast majority of women with stomas can conceive and experience completely normal pregnancies. Stomas don't affect fertility, so conception occurs without additional problems.\n\n**First Trimester: Managing Nausea**\nDuring the first months, morning sickness requires specific strategies. Keep dry crackers and water always within reach to manage discomfort. \nIt's preferable to change the collection system in the evening when nausea is less intense, and use smaller bags during the day for greater comfort. Hydration control becomes fundamental and requires regular medical monitoring.\n\n**Second and Third Trimester: Physical Adaptations**\nAs the belly grows, stoma size may increase but will return to normal after delivery. Use more flexible baseplates that adapt to body changes and invest in support belts specific for pregnant ostomates. The stoma may disappear from view as the belly grows, so using a mirror becomes helpful. Apply barrier creams to protect more sensitive skin and document changes with photos to help the medical team.\n\n**Delivery and Breastfeeding**\nMost women deliver without stoma-related complications. It's essential to prepare extra hospital supplies and inform all healthcare staff about the stoma presence. \nDuring breastfeeding, experimenting with different positions helps find the most comfortable one. Often the side-lying position avoids pressure on the stoma while maintaining good baby latch.\n\nHaving a stoma is not an obstacle to motherhood. With proper preparation and medical support, every woman can fully experience pregnancy and parenthood, often discovering unexpected strength and resilience."
          }
        },
        "es-419" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "**\"Existe la suposición de que si tienes una estoma no podrás tener un bebé\",** dice Holly Fleet, de 28 años, quien enfrentó un embarazo después de una ileostomía de emergencia en 2021.\nLa realidad es muy diferente: la gran mayoría de las mujeres con estoma pueden concebir y tener embarazos completamente normales. El estoma no afecta la fertilidad, por lo que la concepción ocurre sin problemas adicionales.\n\n**Primer trimestre: control de las náuseas**\nDurante los primeros meses, las náuseas matutinas requieren estrategias específicas. Manteniendo siempre a la mano galletas saladas y agua para aliviar el malestar.\nEs preferible cambiar el sistema de recolección por la noche, cuando las náuseas son menos intensas, y usando bolsas más pequeñas durante el día para mayor comodidad. El control de la hidratación se vuelve fundamental y requiere monitoreo médico regular.\n\n**Segundo y tercer trimestre: adaptaciones físicas**\nA medida que el abdomen crece, el tamaño del estoma puede aumentar, pero volverá a la normalidad después del parto. Usando placas base más flexibles que se adapten a los cambios del cuerpo e invirtiendo en cinturones de soporte específicos para embarazadas con estoma. El estoma puede dejar de estar visible conforme crece la panza, por lo que usar un espejo resulta útil. Aplicando cremas barrera para proteger la piel, que se vuelve más sensible, y documentando los cambios con fotos para ayudar al equipo médico.\n\n**Parto y lactancia**\nLa mayoría de las mujeres da a luz sin complicaciones relacionadas con el estoma. Es esencial preparar suministros adicionales para el hospital e informar a todo el personal médico sobre la presencia del estoma.\nDurante la lactancia, ayuda experimentar con diferentes posiciones para encontrar la más cómoda. A menudo, la posición acostada de lado evita la presión sobre el estoma y permite un buen agarre del bebé.\n\nTener un estoma no es un obstáculo para la maternidad. Con la preparación adecuada y el apoyo médico, toda mujer puede vivir plenamente el embarazo y la maternidad, descubriendo con frecuencia una fortaleza y resiliencia inesperadas.\n"
          }
        }
      }
    },
